WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.403 --> 00:00:03.353
Kodak invented one of the world's first digital cameras.

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.288
Yet decades later, the company that helped create the future

3
00:00:06.368 --> 00:00:08.121
of photography filed for bankruptcy.

4
00:00:08.626 --> 00:00:11.498
For decades, Kodak was a giant in photography.

5
00:00:11.646 --> 00:00:13.626
People bought its cameras, film rolls,

6
00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:14.931
and printing services.

7
00:00:15.066 --> 00:00:18.131
Its business was built around capturing memories on film.

8
00:00:19.446 --> 00:00:23.601
Then, in 1975, Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built a digital

9
00:00:23.681 --> 00:00:27.630
camera prototype. It was bulky, captured tiny black-and-white images,

10
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.314
and took 23 seconds to record a photograph.

11
00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:36.406
But digital photography challenged the economics of Kodak's established film

12
00:00:36.486 --> 00:00:37.106
business.

13
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.473
The company had to navigate a difficult transition while protecting

14
00:00:40.553 --> 00:00:42.332
an enormous existing business.

15
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:48.583
Kodak tried multiple strategies, but its financial and competitive pressures

16
00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:53.677
continued. In January 2012, the company filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy

17
00:00:53.757 --> 00:00:59.546
protection. The lesson isn't that Kodak never innovated. It did.

18
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.875
The deeper problem was turning innovation into a sustainable business

19
00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:06.186
model before the market moved beyond its traditional strengths.

20
00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.895
Because inventing the future is one thing.

21
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.857
Building a business that can survive it is another.
